T30.01|A personal balance sheet lists assets and liabilities at a date; net worth is their difference. A personal cash-flow statement tracks receipts and payments over a period. High net worth does not necessarily provide cash for near-term bills when wealth is locked in property or a business.~~Use realistic values and distinguish accessible savings from restricted or illiquid assets. Do not count the same retirement or investment account twice.|Assets: Owned resources with estimated value.;;Liabilities: Outstanding financial obligations.;;Cash flow: Income and payments through time.|A household owns ₹80 lakh property and ₹10 savings, and owes ₹35 mortgage plus ₹5 other debt.~~Assets are ₹90.;;Liabilities are ₹40.;;Net worth is ₹50, but liquid savings are only ₹10.~~Wealth and accessible liquidity are different.~~Property values and selling costs may change realised net worth.|Use consistent valuation dates.;;Separate liquid and illiquid assets.;;Track monthly cash as well as net worth.
T30.02|A budget plans how income serves spending, debt, saving and future goals. Fixed commitments, variable needs and discretionary choices behave differently under income shocks. A useful budget incorporates irregular expenses rather than treating predictable annual bills as surprises.~~Compare actual results with the plan and adjust assumptions. A spreadsheet surplus that ignores cash payment dates may still leave a temporary shortfall.|Essential costs: Necessary ongoing spending.;;Discretionary costs: Flexible choices.;;Sinking funds: Planned provision for irregular expenses.|Monthly income is ₹80,000; routine spending ₹50,000, debt payments ₹15,000 and annual insurance ₹60,000.~~Set aside ₹5,000 monthly for the annual bill.;;Remaining planned saving is ₹10,000.;;Check when the premium is due relative to accumulated funds.~~Irregular expenses reduce the apparent surplus.~~Income uncertainty and other commitments need additional planning.|Include annual costs.;;Distinguish commitments from preferences.;;Reconcile budget with actual cash dates.
T30.03|An emergency fund provides accessible resources for unexpected essential costs or income interruptions. Its size depends on obligations, income stability, dependants and alternative support. It serves resilience rather than maximum investment return.~~Accessibility and value stability matter together. A risky asset that can be sold quickly may still be worth much less when an emergency occurs.|Income buffer: Supports spending during interruption.;;Expense buffer: Covers unforeseen essential costs.;;Liquidity buffer: Avoids forced sales or expensive borrowing.|Essential monthly outflow is ₹50,000; a household tests a four-month interruption.~~The illustrative spending need is ₹2 lakh.;;Add known one-off risks separately.;;Compare with truly accessible resources and support.~~The required buffer follows the scenario and circumstances.~~Four months is an assumption here, not a universal recommendation.|Quantify essential expenses.;;Stress income interruptions.;;Check access, restrictions and value stability.
T30.04|Debt management considers interest, fees, repayment dates and consequences of default. Credit scores summarise aspects of borrowing history using provider-specific models; they are not a complete measure of financial health or affordability. Timely repayment can matter even for a low balance.~~Compare effective borrowing cost and contractual terms. A lower monthly payment may simply extend the term and increase total interest.|Revolving debt: Balance can roll subject to terms.;;Instalment debt: Scheduled repayment over a term.;;Credit history: Recorded borrowing and repayment behaviour.|A ₹1 lakh balance incurs assumed 2% monthly interest with no repayment.~~After one month it becomes ₹1.02 lakh.;;After twelve months, about ₹1.268 lakh.;;Compare an actual repayment plan and fees.~~Monthly compounding makes annual cost exceed 24%.~~Actual cost and score effects depend on the lender and local rules.|List rates and due dates.;;Compare total borrowing cost.;;Check reports for errors through official channels.
T30.05|Education, vehicle and housing loans fund different assets or future benefits, but all create cash commitments. Affordability should include payment changes, related ownership costs and income uncertainty. Collateral or an expected salary increase does not guarantee repayment capacity.~~Read rate-reset, grace-period, prepayment and security terms. Borrowing cost is not just the advertised interest rate.|Education loan: Funds study with defined repayment terms.;;Vehicle loan: Finances a depreciating asset.;;Housing loan: Long-term property-backed obligation.|Income is ₹1 lakh monthly; an illustrative housing payment is ₹35,000 and associated costs ₹10,000.~~Total housing cash commitment is ₹45,000.;;A 20% income decline raises its income share from 45% to 56.25%.;;Test other essential expenses and rate changes.~~Initial payment ratios can understate downside pressure.~~This is a scenario, not a prescribed affordability threshold.|Include related costs.;;Stress income and rates.;;Compare effective cost and contractual flexibility.
T30.06|Goal-based saving specifies the amount, date and importance of future needs. Required contributions depend on starting capital and uncertain returns; a desired return should not be inserted merely to make a plan look affordable. Essential and flexible goals may justify different risk choices.~~Inflation affects the future price of a goal. Review contributions when costs or circumstances change rather than treating the first forecast as fixed.|Near-term goal: Funds needed soon.;;Long-term goal: More time but uncertain investment outcomes.;;Priority goal: Essential versus adjustable commitment.|A goal costs ₹10 lakh today and is due in five years; assume 5% annual price inflation.~~Future cost is about ₹12.76 lakh.;;With no investment return and no starting funds, saving is about ₹21,271 monthly over 60 months.;;Explore realistic return and timing scenarios separately.~~Saving needs depend on both goal inflation and investment assumptions.~~No return is guaranteed.|State amount and date.;;Rank essential goals.;;Review contributions and adverse scenarios.
T30.07|Insurance-needs analysis identifies financial consequences a household cannot comfortably absorb. Dependants, debts, medical costs and existing resources affect required protection. Product selection follows the risk gap, not the attractiveness of a sales illustration.~~Avoid double-counting resources or assuming employer cover continues after job loss. Savings and insurance perform related but different roles.|Life need: Dependants and obligations after death.;;Health need: Eligible medical-cost exposure.;;Asset/liability need: Property loss or claims by others.|A family estimates ₹40 lakh support needs and ₹20 debt, with ₹15 usable assets and ₹10 existing relevant cover.~~Gross need is ₹60.;;Resources total ₹25.;;Illustrative uncovered gap is ₹35.~~Protection follows the obligations and available resources.~~Future income, policy exclusions and inflation require fuller analysis.|Map financial consequences.;;Check existing coverage and portability.;;Separate protection from investment returns.
T30.08|Investment selection begins with goals, horizon, liquidity and loss capacity, then considers asset allocation and implementation. Diversification depends on underlying risks rather than the number of products. Costs, taxes and product access can materially change outcomes.~~Evaluate suitability at the household level. Owning several funds focused on the same sector may create one concentrated bet.|Asset allocation: Distribution across economic risk classes.;;Implementation: Instruments used for the allocation.;;Portfolio fit: How a holding changes total exposure.|A household invests ₹10 lakh in each of three funds; all hold mainly the same industry.~~Total exposure is ₹30 lakh.;;Look through to shared holdings and drivers.;;Compare that concentration with goals and risk capacity.~~Three products do not necessarily provide three independent exposures.~~No particular allocation is recommended by this example.|Start with goals and constraints.;;Look through holdings.;;Compare costs, risks and liquidity.
T30.09|Tax-efficient investing evaluates after-tax cash outcomes using the investor's actual country, residency, account and period. Deferral, exemptions, credits and different income categories can matter, but conditions and limits change. A tax advantage alone does not make an unsuitable investment appropriate.~~Compare equivalent economic exposure and total costs. Tax treatment should be verified from current primary rules before implementation.|Tax deferral: Delays payment under eligible rules.;;Tax exemption: Removes specified taxable income under conditions.;;Tax-aware realisation: Considers timing and character of gains.|Two hypothetical investments earn 8% gross; one faces assumed 25% tax on that return, the other is tax-exempt but charges 2.5% yearly.~~First net return is approximately 6%.;;Second is approximately 5.5% before other effects.;;Compare risk and conditions as well as tax.~~Tax-free does not automatically mean higher net return.~~Rates are invented for teaching, not current law.|Verify local rules.;;Include fees and restrictions.;;Compare after-tax scenarios on equivalent risk.
T30.10|Retirement planning connects contributions, investment uncertainty and future spending. Withdrawals depend on inflation, longevity, taxes and the sequence of returns. A constant average return does not describe the risk of losses early in retirement while assets are being sold.~~No withdrawal rate guarantees success across all horizons and portfolios. Model adverse sequences and adjustable spending alongside the base case.|Accumulation: Builds resources before retirement.;;Decumulation: Funds spending from assets.;;Sequence risk: Return order changes outcomes with withdrawals.|Start with ₹100 lakh and withdraw ₹10 at each year-end; returns are −20% then +25%.~~Year one ends at ₹70.;;Year two ends at ₹77.5.;;Reverse returns: balances become ₹115 then ₹82.~~Identical returns in different order produce different ending wealth.~~The example excludes tax and inflation.|Use long-horizon spending scenarios.;;Test early losses.;;Review spending flexibility and guaranteed income terms.
T30.11|Inflation risk is loss of purchasing power; longevity risk is living longer than funded resources support. They interact because a longer retirement exposes spending to more years of rising costs. Nominal asset growth should be compared with inflation and fees.~~Planning should distinguish essential spending from flexible spending and consider how different income sources respond to inflation.|Purchasing-power risk: Prices rise faster than resources.;;Longevity risk: Spending horizon exceeds expectations.;;Real return: Growth after inflation adjustment.|A portfolio earns 6% while prices rise 4%.~~Exact real return is 1.06 ÷ 1.04 − 1 ≈ 1.92%.;;A ₹5 lakh annual expense grows to about ₹10.96 after twenty years at 4%.;;Test a longer funding horizon.~~Nominal gains can provide modest real progress.~~Actual personal inflation can differ from a broad index.|Model real spending.;;Use multiple longevity horizons.;;Check income indexation and flexibility.
T30.12|Compensation can include salary, bonuses, restricted shares and options, each with different certainty, timing and tax treatment. Employer equity also concentrates financial wealth in the same company supporting employment income. Quoted grant value is not necessarily available cash.~~Vesting, exercise costs, expiry and trading restrictions matter. Evaluate the combined exposure of income and investment rather than considering stock in isolation.|Cash compensation: Pay available under employment terms.;;Restricted equity: Ownership subject to conditions.;;Options: Right to buy at a specified price.|An employee has options on 10,000 shares at ₹50 exercise price; market price is ₹70.~~Intrinsic value is ₹2 lakh.;;Exercise requires ₹5 lakh cash.;;Assess tax, vesting and sale restrictions separately.~~Positive option value does not eliminate funding needs.~~Price falls can reduce or remove intrinsic value.|Read award documents.;;Measure employer concentration.;;Plan exercise cash and verify current tax rules.
T30.13|Buying versus renting compares the full economic and lifestyle consequences of housing choices. Ownership includes interest, maintenance, transaction costs and capital tied up; renting includes rent changes and moving risk. Principal repayment builds equity and should not be treated exactly like interest expense.~~Use equivalent homes and realistic holding periods. Appreciation assumptions and opportunity cost can dominate a comparison.|Ownership cost: Financing, upkeep and transaction costs.;;Rental cost: Rent and associated obligations.;;Opportunity cost: Return forgone on committed capital.|A home's annual cash payment is ₹8 lakh, including ₹3 principal, plus ₹2 upkeep; annual rent is ₹6.~~Ownership cash outflow is ₹10.;;Non-principal cash cost is ₹7 before other items.;;Compare deposit opportunity cost and sale proceeds over the horizon.~~Cash affordability and economic cost are different comparisons.~~No future appreciation is assumed.|Compare equivalent housing.;;Separate principal from expense.;;Include transaction costs and flexibility.
T30.14|Family financial planning coordinates shared goals, responsibilities and risks across household members. Education, caregiving, housing and dependants can compete for the same resources. Communication and access to key records are part of financial resilience.~~Do not assume one person's preferences or income will remain constant. Plans should consider disability, separation, caregiving changes and other realistic disruptions.|Shared goals: Jointly prioritised commitments.;;Responsibility map: Who manages payments and records.;;Contingency plan: Actions after income or family changes.|A household has ₹20 lakh savings and goals requiring ₹12 for education and ₹15 for housing.~~Combined goal needs are ₹27.;;Funding gap is ₹7 before returns and timing.;;Prioritise, adjust dates or increase saving explicitly.~~The same savings cannot fully fund both goals.~~Different dates and support resources may change the plan.|Avoid double-counting funds.;;Agree priorities and responsibilities.;;Maintain accessible contingency records.
T30.15|Estate planning addresses ownership, authority and transfer of assets after incapacity or death. Wills, trusts, nominations and beneficiary designations have different legal effects across jurisdictions and asset types. A nomination should not be assumed universally to override inheritance law.~~Accurate records and consistent documents reduce administrative uncertainty. Legal advice and current local rules are necessary for implementation.|Will: Directs estate distribution under applicable law.;;Trust: Holds assets under specified legal arrangements.;;Nomination/beneficiary: Records a designated recipient or administrator under relevant rules.|A person's account nomination differs from their will.~~Identify the account's legal framework.;;Determine the effect of each document under local law.;;Update inconsistent records with qualified advice.~~Document labels alone cannot settle ownership rights.~~The example illustrates the need for reconciliation, not a legal outcome.|Inventory assets and liabilities.;;Check current local law.;;Keep documents and beneficiary records consistent.
T30.16|Wealth preservation combines spending discipline, diversification, liquidity, governance and succession. Intergenerational transfer can alter incentives and control as well as ownership. Concentrated family business wealth needs an explicit plan for reinvestment, distributions and conflict resolution.~~Preservation does not mean avoiding all risk: inflation and excessive conservatism can also erode real resources. Define what wealth is intended to support.|Capital preservation: Protects resources against severe loss.;;Real preservation: Maintains purchasing power.;;Family governance: Defines decisions and succession.|A family has ₹100 lakh wealth, spends ₹8 annually and earns ₹5 before inflation.~~Nominal capital falls to ₹97 after one year.;;Inflation further reduces purchasing power.;;Review spending, risk and income assumptions together.~~A positive investment return can coexist with wealth depletion.~~Taxes and irregular obligations add further effects.|Define purpose and spending rules.;;Diversify common dependencies.;;Document succession and decision rights.
T30.17|Financial scams exploit urgency, authority, secrecy or unrealistic promises to obtain money or information. Verification should use independently found official channels rather than contact details supplied in the message. A small successful withdrawal can be used to build trust in a fraudulent scheme.~~Protect credentials and payment approvals; legitimate-looking branding is not evidence of authorisation. If fraud occurs, prompt action through the bank and official reporting channels can matter.|Impersonation: Pretends to be a trusted organisation.;;Investment fraud: Fabricates returns or asset ownership.;;Payment/credential fraud: Redirects money or steals access.|A message promises 3% guaranteed monthly returns and requests an urgent transfer.~~Annual compounding would imply about 42.6%.;;Verify the entity independently with relevant official registers.;;Do not rely on screenshots or testimonials as proof.~~Extreme promises and pressure are reasons to investigate.~~The arithmetic alone cannot determine whether a specific entity is fraudulent.|Verify independently.;;Protect credentials and approvals.;;Use official reporting routes promptly after suspected loss.
T30.18|Financial advisers may charge fixed fees, hourly fees, asset-based fees or receive commissions. Compensation can create incentives that should be understood alongside competence, authorisation and service scope. Disclosure does not automatically eliminate a conflict.~~Compare total cost and what the adviser is responsible for. Investment management, planning, product distribution and tax or legal advice are different services.|Fee-only arrangement: Paid through agreed client fees.;;Commission arrangement: Paid through product transactions.;;Asset-based arrangement: Charge related to managed assets.|An adviser charges 1% on ₹50 lakh plus a recommended fund charging 1.5%.~~Adviser charge is ₹50,000 yearly.;;Fund expense is about ₹75,000 at that asset level.;;Approximate combined recurring cost is ₹1.25 lakh before other charges.~~Layered costs affect net outcomes.~~Actual fee bases and compounding require the contracts.|Verify authorisation and scope.;;Request total cost disclosure.;;Ask how recommendations and conflicts are managed.
